  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw4o-density-shapes">
    <title>Which Shapes Can Carry This Density?</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        With <m>\delta(x,y) = x^2-y^2</m> and the shapes of
        <xref ref="fig-hw4o-shapes"/>: type the numbers of the two shapes
        that can carry <m>\delta</m> as a density, in increasing order and
        separated by a comma (for instance <c>1,3</c>).
      </p>
      <p>Answer: <var width="8"/></p>
      <p>Which of those two is heavier? Type its number.</p>
      <p>Answer: <var width="6"/></p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="2\s*,\s*4">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string="1\s*,\s*3">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="2">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string="4">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
        A density cannot be negative, so we need
        <m>x^2 - y^2 \ge 0</m>, that is <m>|x| \ge |y|</m>. That holds on
        shapes <m>2</m> and <m>4</m>, and fails on shapes <m>1</m> and
        <m>3</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Shape <m>2</m> is the triangle of area <m>r^2/2</m> and shape
        <m>4</m> the sector of area <m>\pi r^2/8</m>. The triangle
        contains the sector's range of radii and has the larger area, so
        <m>\iint_{R_2}\delta\,dA \ge \iint_{R_4}\delta\,dA</m>: shape
        <m>2</m> is heavier.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw4o-reverse-order">
    <title>Reversing the Order of Integration</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Rewrite <m>\displaystyle\int_0^5\!\!\int_{x/2}^{x}
        f(x,y)\,dy\,dx</m> with the order of integration reversed.
      </p>
      <p>
        How many double integrals does the reversed order need?
        <var width="6"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        Taking them in order of increasing <m>y</m>: the first runs up to
        <m>y = </m> <var width="6"/>, and in it <m>x</m> goes from <m>y</m>
        to <var width="8"/>; in the second, <m>x</m> goes from <m>y</m> to
        <var width="6"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var>
        <condition number="2" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition number="1" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="2.5" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
              <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
                <p>Incorrect.</p>
              </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="2\s*\*?\s*y">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
              <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
                <p>Incorrect.</p>
              </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="5" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
              <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
                <p>Incorrect.</p>
              </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
        The region is bounded by <m>y = x/2</m> below, <m>y = x</m> above,
        and <m>x = 5</m> on the right. Reading it in <m>y</m>-slices, the
        left boundary is always <m>x = y</m>; the right boundary is
        <m>x = 2y</m> until <m>2y = 5</m>, and <m>x = 5</m> after that:
      </p>
      <me>
        \int_0^{5/2}\!\!\int_{y}^{2y} f\,dx\,dy
        + \int_{5/2}^{5}\!\!\int_{y}^{5} f\,dx\,dy .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw4o-volume">
    <title>A Volume Over a Triangle</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the volume under <m>z = \sqrt{1-x^2}</m> and above the
        triangle formed by <m>y=x</m>, <m>x=1</m>, and the <m>x</m>-axis.
      </p>
      <p>
        After integrating in <m>y</m>, the remaining integrand is
        <var width="12"/> (in terms of <m>x</m>), and the volume is
        <var width="10"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="x\s*\*?\s*sqrt\s*\(\s*1\s*-\s*x\s*\^\s*2\s*\)">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
                <condition string=".*">
            <feedback>
                  <p>Incorrect.</p>
                </feedback>
          </condition>
        </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="0.333" tolerance="0.005">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
              <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
                <p>Incorrect.</p>
              </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
        The triangle is <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>, <m>0 \le y \le x</m>, so
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          V \amp = \int_0^1\!\!\int_0^x \sqrt{1-x^2}\,dy\,dx
          = \int_0^1 x\sqrt{1-x^2}\,dx
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \left[-\tfrac13\left(1-x^2\right)^{3/2}\right]_0^1
          = \frac13 .
        </mrow>
      </md>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <!-- The eight problems below come from a Math 53 discussion worksheet      -->
  <!-- (Discussion 12, double integrals; UC Berkeley, Spring 2021,           -->
  <!-- Prof. Stankova): its problems 1.1, 1.2, 2(a), 2(c), 3(c), 3(e), 3(f)  -->
  <!-- and 5(c), with the worksheet's own solutions, typeset here.           -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw4o-geo-box">
    <title>A Volume You Can See</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use a geometric argument to find the value of
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_{[0,a]\times[0,b]} c\,dA</m>, where
        <m>a</m>, <m>b</m> and <m>c</m> are positive constants.
      </p>
      <p>
        The integral is the volume of what solid? Type one word.
        <var width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        Its value, in terms of <m>a</m>, <m>b</m> and <m>c</m> (use
        <c>*</c> for products): <var width="10"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string=".*(box|prism|cuboid|parallelepiped|brick).*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="([abc])\s*\*?\s*(?!\1)([abc])\s*\*?\s*(?!\1|\2)[abc]">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
        Over the rectangle <m>[0,a]\times[0,b]</m> the graph of the
        integrand is the plane <m>z = c</m>, at constant height <m>c</m>.
        The integral is therefore the volume of a rectangular box with side
        lengths <m>a</m>, <m>b</m> and <m>c</m>:
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_{[0,a]\times[0,b]} c\,dA = abc .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw4o-geo-half-ball">
    <title>Half a Ball</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use a geometric argument to find the value of
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_{x^2+y^2\le 1} \sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}\,dA</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The integral is the volume of what solid? <var width="14"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        Its value: <var width="10"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string=".*(hemisphere|half.*ball|half.*sphere|semi.?sphere|half.*globe).*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="2.0944" tolerance="0.005">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
        Over the unit disk <m>x^2+y^2\le 1</m>, the graph of
        <m>z = \sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}</m> is the upper half of the unit sphere
        <m>x^2+y^2+z^2 = 1</m>. The integral is therefore the volume of the
        upper half of the unit ball. A ball of radius <m>R</m> has volume
        <m>\tfrac43\pi R^3</m>, so
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_{x^2+y^2\le 1} \sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}\,dA
        = \frac12\cdot\frac43\pi = \frac{2\pi}{3} \approx 2.094 .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw4o-order-triangle">
    <title>Changing the Order: a Triangle</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Change the order of integration in
        <m>\displaystyle\int_0^1\!\!\int_0^y f(x,y)\,dx\,dy</m>. Sketching
        the region of integration might be helpful.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the new order, <m>dy\,dx</m>: the outer integral in <m>x</m>
        runs from <var width="6"/> to <var width="6"/>, and the inner
        integral in <m>y</m> runs from <var width="6"/> to
        <var width="6"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var>
        <condition number="0" tolerance="0.001">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="1" tolerance="0.001">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="x">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="1" tolerance="0.001">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
        The region is <m>0 \le y \le 1</m>, <m>0 \le x \le y</m>: the
        triangle with vertices <m>(0,0)</m>, <m>(0,1)</m> and <m>(1,1)</m>.
        Described the other way it is <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>,
        <m>x \le y \le 1</m>, so
      </p>
      <me>
        \int_0^1\!\!\int_0^y f(x,y)\,dx\,dy
        = \int_0^1\!\!\int_x^1 f(x,y)\,dy\,dx .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw4o-order-log">
    <title>Changing the Order: Under a Logarithm</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Change the order of integration in
        <m>\displaystyle\int_1^2\!\!\int_0^{\ln x} f(x,y)\,dy\,dx</m>.
        Sketching the region of integration might be helpful.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the new order, <m>dx\,dy</m>: the outer integral in <m>y</m>
        runs from <var width="6"/> to <var width="8"/>, and the inner
        integral in <m>x</m> runs from <var width="8"/> to
        <var width="6"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var>
        <condition number="0" tolerance="0.001">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="0.6931" tolerance="0.005">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="e\s*\^\s*\(?\s*y\s*\)?|exp\s*\(\s*y\s*\)">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="2" tolerance="0.001">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
        The region is <m>1 \le x \le 2</m>, <m>0 \le y \le \ln x</m>.
        Since <m>\ln</m> is increasing, <m>y</m> ranges over
        <m>0 \le y \le \ln 2</m>, and <m>y \le \ln x</m> is the same as
        <m>e^y \le x</m>. So
      </p>
      <me>
        \int_1^2\!\!\int_0^{\ln x} f(x,y)\,dy\,dx
        = \int_0^{\ln 2}\!\!\int_{e^y}^{2} f(x,y)\,dx\,dy .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw4o-x-under-sine">
    <title>Under One Arch of the Sine</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute <m>\displaystyle\iint_D x\,dA</m>, where
        <m>D = \{(x,y) \mid 0 \le x \le \pi,\ 0 \le y \le \sin x\}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        After integrating in <m>y</m>, the remaining integrand is
        <var width="12"/> (in terms of <m>x</m>), and the integral equals
        <var width="10"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="x\s*\*?\s*sin\s*\(?\s*x\s*\)?|sin\s*\(?\s*x\s*\)?\s*\*?\s*x">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="3.1416" tolerance="0.005">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
        <m>D</m> is a region of type I, so
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \iint_D x\,dA \amp = \int_0^{\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\sin x} x\,dy\,dx
          = \int_0^{\pi} x\sin x\,dx
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \Bigl[-x\cos x\Bigr]_0^{\pi} + \int_0^{\pi} \cos x\,dx
          = \pi + 0 = \pi \approx 3.142 ,
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        integrating by parts with <m>u = x</m>, <m>dv = \sin x\,dx</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw4o-exp-square">
    <title>An Integrand With No Antiderivative</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute <m>\displaystyle\int_0^1\!\!\int_{4y}^{4} e^{x^2}\,dx\,dy</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        With the order of integration reversed, the outer integral in
        <m>x</m> runs from <m>0</m> to <m>4</m>, and the inner integral in
        <m>y</m> runs from <m>0</m> to <var width="8"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The integral equals <var width="14"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="x\s*/\s*4|0?\.25\s*\*?\s*x|\(\s*1\s*/\s*4\s*\)\s*\*?\s*x|x\s*\*\s*0?\.25">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="1110763.69" tolerance="0.5">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
        The function <m>e^{x^2}</m> has no antiderivative that can be
        written with familiar functions, so change the order of
        integration. The region <m>0 \le y \le 1</m>,
        <m>4y \le x \le 4</m> is the same as <m>0 \le x \le 4</m>,
        <m>0 \le y \le x/4</m>. Hence, with the substitution
        <m>s = x^2</m>, <m>ds = 2x\,dx</m>,
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_0^1\!\!\int_{4y}^{4} e^{x^2}\,dx\,dy
          \amp = \int_0^4\!\!\int_0^{x/4} e^{x^2}\,dy\,dx
          = \int_0^4 \frac{x}{4}\,e^{x^2}\,dx
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \frac18\int_0^{16} e^{s}\,ds
          = \frac{e^{16}-1}{8} \approx 1110763.690 .
        </mrow>
      </md>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw4o-arcsin">
    <title>Reversing to Tame the Inner Integral</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute
        <m>\displaystyle\int_0^1\!\!\int_{\arcsin y}^{\pi/2}
        \cos x\,\sqrt{1+\cos^2 x}\,dx\,dy</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        With the order of integration reversed, the outer integral in
        <m>x</m> runs from <m>0</m> to <m>\pi/2</m>, and the inner integral
        in <m>y</m> runs from <m>0</m> to <var width="8"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The integral equals <var width="10"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="sin\s*\(?\s*x\s*\)?">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="0.6095" tolerance="0.005">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
        The inner integral looks hard, so change the order of
        integration. The region is the rectangle
        <m>[0,\pi/2]\times[0,1]</m> with the extra condition
        <m>\arcsin y \le x</m>, which is the same as <m>y \le \sin x</m>.
        Hence
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_0^1\!\!\int_{\arcsin y}^{\pi/2} \cos x\,\sqrt{1+\cos^2 x}\,dx\,dy
          \amp = \int_0^{\pi/2}\!\!\int_0^{\sin x} \cos x\,\sqrt{1+\cos^2 x}\,dy\,dx
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \int_0^{\pi/2} \sin x\cos x\,\sqrt{1+\cos^2 x}\,dx
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \frac12\int_0^1 \sqrt{1+s}\,ds
          = \frac13\left(2\sqrt2-1\right) \approx 0.609 ,
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        with the substitution <m>s = \cos^2 x</m>,
        <m>ds = -2\sin x\cos x\,dx</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw4o-fubini-tf">
    <title>Did Fubini Lie to Us?</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        True or false? Supply convincing reasoning for your answer.
      </p>
      <me>
        \int_0^1\!\!\int_0^1 \frac{x^2-y^2}{\left(x^2+y^2\right)^2}\,dy\,dx
        = \int_0^1\!\!\int_0^1 \frac{x^2-y^2}{\left(x^2+y^2\right)^2}\,dx\,dy
        \quad\text{by Fubini's theorem.}
      </me>
      <p>
        <em>Hint:</em>
        <m>\dfrac{d}{dy}\,\dfrac{y}{x^2+y^2}
        = \dfrac{x^2-y^2}{\left(x^2+y^2\right)^2}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Type <c>true</c> or <c>false</c>: <var width="8"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        The left-hand side (<m>dy\,dx</m>) equals <var width="10"/>, and the
        right-hand side (<m>dx\,dy</m>) equals <var width="10"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="false">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="0.7854" tolerance="0.005">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="-0.7854" tolerance="0.005">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw4-solutions">
      <p>
        <alert>False.</alert> Compute both sides. Using the hint,
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_0^1\!\!\int_0^1 \frac{x^2-y^2}{\left(x^2+y^2\right)^2}\,dy\,dx
          \amp = \int_0^1 \left[\frac{y}{x^2+y^2}\right]_{y=0}^{y=1} dx
          = \int_0^1 \frac{dx}{1+x^2}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \arctan 1 - \arctan 0 = \frac{\pi}{4} .
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        In the other order, <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\,\dfrac{-x}{x^2+y^2}
        = \dfrac{x^2-y^2}{\left(x^2+y^2\right)^2}</m>, so
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_0^1\!\!\int_0^1 \frac{x^2-y^2}{\left(x^2+y^2\right)^2}\,dx\,dy
          \amp = \int_0^1 \left[\frac{-x}{x^2+y^2}\right]_{x=0}^{x=1} dy
          = \int_0^1 \frac{-dy}{1+y^2}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = -\arctan 1 + \arctan 0 = -\frac{\pi}{4} .
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        The two orders give different answers, and Fubini's theorem is not
        contradicted: it requires the integrand to be continuous on the
        rectangle, and this one is unbounded near <m>(0,0)</m>, so it is
        not continuous on <m>[0,1]\times[0,1]</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>
